WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:11.700
Today you are going to see how Google’s new&nbsp;
AI just supercharged art generation. Again!

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:18.540
Yes, this year, we are entering the age&nbsp;
of AI-based art generation. OpenAI’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.620
DALL-E 2 technique is able to&nbsp;
take a piece of text from us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.300
and generate a stunning image&nbsp;
that matches this description.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:34.620
Stable Diffusion, a similar, but open source&nbsp;
technique is also now available for everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:41.880
to use. And the results are so good that artists&nbsp;
are already using it around the world to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:48.840
illustrations for a novel, texture synthesis&nbsp;
for virtual worlds, product design, and more.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.640
So, are we done here? Is there&nbsp;
nothing else to improve other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.960
than the visual fidelity of&nbsp;
the results? Well, not quite!

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.540
Have a look at two of my favorite&nbsp;
AI-generated images, this scholar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:09.360
who is desperately trying to hold on to his&nbsp;
papers. So, I am very happy with this image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:16.380
but imagine that if we were creating a comic,&nbsp;
we would need more images of this chap doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.840
other things. Can we do that? Well, we&nbsp;
are experienced Fellow Scholars here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.840
so, we know that variant generation comes&nbsp;
to the rescue, right? Well, have a look.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.820
Clearly, the AI has an understanding of the image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:39.660
and can create somewhat similar images: let’s&nbsp;
see - we get someone with a similar beard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:46.920
similar paper which is similarly on fire.&nbsp;
The fact that the AI can have a look at such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:54.120
an image and create variants is a miracle of&nbsp;
science…but not quite what we are looking for.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.060
Why is that? Well, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:03.240
this is a new scholar. We are looking for&nbsp;
the previous scholar doing other things.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:12.120
Now let’s try our fox scientist too and see if&nbsp;
this was maybe just an anomaly? Maybe DALL-E 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:20.880
is just not into our Scholarly content? Let’s see!&nbsp;
Well, once again, the results are pretty good. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:28.080
understood that the huge ears, gloves, labcoat&nbsp;
and the tie are important elements of the image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.660
but ultimately, this is a different&nbsp;
fox scientist in a different style.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.080
So, no more adventures for these scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.860
right? Have we lost them&nbsp;
forever? Should we give up?

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:47.580
Well, not so fast! Have a look at Google’s new&nbsp;
technique, which promises a solution to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:54.240
challenging problem! Yes, they promise that if we&nbsp;
are able to take about 4 images of our subject,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.580
for instance, this good boy here, it&nbsp;
will be able to synthesize completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:06.060
new images with them. Let’s see, this&nbsp;
is the same dog in the Acropolis.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:14.640
That is excellent. However… wait a minute! This&nbsp;
photo is very similar to this one. So basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.120
there was a little synthesis going on here,&nbsp;
but otherwise, changing the background carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.700
the show here. That is now new. We can do&nbsp;
that with already existing tools anyway.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:32.580
So, is that it? Well, hold on to your&nbsp;
papers, because the answer is no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:39.120
it goes beyond changing backgrounds. It&nbsp;
goes so far beyond that that I don’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.700
know where to start! For instance, here&nbsp;
is our little doggy swimming, sleeping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:53.880
in a bucket, and we can even give this good boy&nbsp;
a haircut. That is absolutely insane. And all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:01.320
these new situations were synthesized by using&nbsp;
only 4 input photos. Wow. Now we’re talking!

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:07.800
Similarly, if we have a pair of stylish&nbsp;
sunglasses, we can ask a bear to wear it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:14.940
make a cool product photo out of it, or put&nbsp;
it in front of the Eiffel Tower. And as I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.760
a light transport researcher by trade, I have&nbsp;
to note that even secondary effects, like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:28.320
reflection of the glasses here are modeled really&nbsp;
well. And so are the reflections here. Loving it.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.920
But, it can do so much more than this. Here&nbsp;
are 5 of my favorite examples from the paper.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:41.580
One, we can not only put our favorite teapot&nbsp;
into different contexts, or see it in use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:47.940
but we can even reimagine an otherwise&nbsp;
opaque object and see what it would look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:53.160
like if it were made of a transparent&nbsp;
material, like glass. I love it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.600
and I bet that product design&nbsp;
people will also love it too.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:04.080
Two, we can create art renditions of our&nbsp;
test subject. Here, the input is only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:10.800
three photos of a dog, but the output, the&nbsp;
output is priceless. We can commission art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:18.120
renditions from legendary artists of the past,&nbsp;
and all this nearly for free. How cool is that?

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:24.120
Three, I hope you liked this teapot property&nbsp;
modification concept, because we are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:28.680
to push it quite a bit further! For&nbsp;
instance, before repainting our car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:34.980
we can have a closer look at what it would look&nbsp;
like, and we can also reimagine our favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.740
little pets as other animals. Which one is&nbsp;
your favorite? Let me know in the comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:46.500
below. Mine is the hippo. It has to be&nbsp;
the hippo. Look at how adorable it is!

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:54.420
And if even that is not enough, four, we can also&nbsp;
ask the AI to reimagine our little dog as a chef,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.420 --> 00:06:01.380
a nurse, a police dog, and many others.&nbsp;
And all of these images are fantastic!

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:09.720
And finally, five it can perform no less than&nbsp;
views synthesis too! We know from previous papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:16.920
that this is quite challenging, and this one only&nbsp;
requests four photos of our cat, and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:23.040
if the cat is refusing to turn the right&nbsp;
direction, which happens basically every time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:30.600
well, no matter, the AI can resynthesize an&nbsp;
image of it looking into our desired directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:37.320
This one looks like something straight out of a&nbsp;
science fiction movie. What a time to be alive!

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.800
And this is a huge step forward, you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.180
previous techniques typically struggled&nbsp;
with this, even in the best cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:52.020
the fidelity of the results suffer. This alarm&nbsp;
clock is not the same as our input photo was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:59.040
And neither is this one. But, with the new&nbsp;
technique, now we’re talking! Bravo Google!

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.440
And once again, don’t forget, the First&nbsp;
Law of Papers is on full display here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:11.160
this huge leap happened just one more paper&nbsp;
down the line. I am truly stunned by these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:17.400
results and I cannot even imagine what we will&nbsp;
be able to do 2 more papers down the line!

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:23.040
So, what do you think? What would you use&nbsp;
this for? Let me know in the comments below!

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.440
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

